Third-party components shipped in the Windows portable bundle
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

whisper.cpp
  Role      : local speech recognition engine (whisper-cli.exe)
  Upstream  : https://github.com/ggml-org/whisper.cpp
  Version   : v1.7.4, commit 8a9ad7844d6e2a10cddf4b92de4089d7ac2b14a9,
              pinned in packaging/windows/model-pack.json
  Licence   : MIT
  Licence   : https://github.com/ggml-org/whisper.cpp/blob/master/LICENSE
  Provenance: compiled from source in this project's release workflow. The
              clone is verified against the pinned commit before anything is
              compiled, so a moved tag cannot change what ships. No prebuilt
              third-party binary is downloaded and none is stored in this
              repository. The commit, its digest and the build flags are
              recorded in whisper/BUILD_MANIFEST.json inside every bundle.

Whisper model weights (ggml conversions)
  Role      : the recognition model, shipped as a separate model pack asset
  Upstream  : https://huggingface.co/ggerganov/whisper.cpp
  Origin    : converted from OpenAI Whisper
  Licence   : MIT (https://github.com/openai/whisper/blob/main/LICENSE)
  Version   : ggml-small-q5_1.bin, 190085487 bytes,
              sha256 ae85e4a935d7a567bd102fe55afc16bb595bdb618e11b2fc7591bc08120411bb,
              fetched from an immutable Hugging Face revision
              (c521a4b02f422512d734391fdf08bb08c0862f68)
  Provenance: downloaded once by the release workflow and checked against the
              digest and byte size pinned in packaging/windows/model-pack.json.
              A mismatch fails the release; there is no override.

              whisper/MODEL_MANIFEST.json inside the pack is an INTEGRITY
              check, not a proof of origin: it travels in the same archive as
              the file it describes, so it can detect truncation and
              corruption but cannot attest to where the bytes came from.
              Provenance rests on the pin in this repository and on the
              release workflow that enforced it. The manifest records whether
              that enforcement actually happened, and an official bundle
              refuses a pack that says it did not.

              The application itself never downloads a model.

Rust crates
  This product statically links around five hundred third-party crates.
  Every one of them, with the licence it declares, is listed in
  packaging/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.json, which is generated from the lockfile
  and checked on every test run: a dependency that appears without a
  recorded licence, or under a licence this project has not reviewed, fails
  the build.

  All of them are permissive. Four carry licences whose notice must travel
  with the distribution, so they are named here rather than left to the
  inventory:

    option-ext              MPL-2.0
      Role     : reached through `dirs`, which resolves the per-user
                 configuration directory.
      Note     : MPL-2.0 is file-level copyleft. The crate is shipped
                 unmodified; were its files ever modified, those changes
                 would have to be published under the MPL.
      Licence  : https://www.mozilla.org/MPL/2.0/

    webpki-roots            CDLA-Permissive-2.0
      Role     : the Mozilla CA root store, used to verify TLS when — and
                 only when — the user presses "Проверить обновления".
      Note     : this is data, not code, and the licence asks for
                 attribution. The root store is Mozilla's; this project
                 neither curates nor modifies it.
      Licence  : https://cdla.dev/permissive-2-0/

    clipboard-win           BSL-1.0
    error-code              BSL-1.0
      Role     : reached through `arboard` on Windows; they are how the
                 clipboard is read and written there.
      Licence  : https://www.boost.org/LICENSE_1_0.txt

  Nothing in the graph is under GPL, AGPL or an LGPL-only licence. One crate
  (`r-efi`) offers LGPL-2.1-or-later as one of three alternatives; this
  project takes the MIT option.

